---
import Landing from '../layouts/Landing.astro';
import AppleMark from '../components/AppleMark.astro';
import { PLAN_LIMITS, PRODUCT_MODELS, PRODUCT_MODEL_INFO } from '@golem/shared';
import { STUDIO_PLUGIN_INSTALL_HREF, STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_LIVE, STUDIO_PLUGIN_STORE_REFUSAL } from '../lib/studio-plugin';
import Horizon from '../components/Horizon.astro';
import ConsentProof from '../components/ConsentProof.astro';
/* The one piece of MODEL OUTPUT on this page. Everything in "What it is good at" above it is drawn
   by this site; this band is a screen the deployed model built, shipped byte-identical from
   docs/evidence, and the door to the other fifteen and the six maps at /showcase. Its own header
   carries the reasons. */
import BuiltScreen from '../components/BuiltScreen.astro';

/*
 * RESTRUCTURED 2026-09-20 AGAINST THE ROSEBUD MEASUREMENT.
 *
 * The owner named rosebud.ai as the bar and said to follow it closely in our own green, keeping our
 * typeface, and to replace the current visual language wherever the two disagree without asking.
 * The measurement is not a memory of the page: packages/corpus/data/ui-references/web-landing.json
 * carries sixteen claims under the id "rosebud-ai-design-language", taken by walking the live DOM
 * with getComputedStyle, and nineteen rules derived from those and the four earlier references.
 * Every structural decision below cites one of them.
 *
 * WHAT THIS FILE OWNS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It owns markup, classes and the geometry of the hero's
 * line art. It defines no CSS: the ramp, the radii, the four control heights, the two transition
 * bands and the accent family all live in landing.css, and this page spends the tokens rather than
 * re-declaring them. Where a new object needs a rule — the model chip, the attachment control, the
 * eyebrow — the class is written as a MODIFIER on a class that already exists (`chip chip-model`,
 * `micro hero-eyebrow`), so the object is styled by the base rule on the day it lands and merely
 * gets better when the sheet names the modifier. An unstyled new class is an invisible object, and
 * this page has shipped one of those before.
 *
 * THE SHAPE, from claims 3, 7, 9, 10, 13 and 14 of that reference:
 *   - a 1024px container behind 32px of gutter, and a short page
 *   - ONE atmosphere layer, fixed, behind everything, at z-index -1 (the sheet makes it fixed; the
 *     container lives inside the hero because that is where its content is authored)
 *   - a hero that is eyebrow, heading, ONE muted line, then the product's own composer as the
 *     focal object — not a picture of a product, the product's own control
 *   - a composer panel at the 8px radius carrying the field, a model chip that names the model
 *     which will answer, an attachment control, and the primary action on the right
 *   - below the fold, every section is a title, one muted line, and then the thing itself
 *
 * WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN, which is the harder half and is unchanged from the first pass.
 * Most of the reference's page area is social proof: play counts, hearts, a populated gallery of
 * other people's games. We have none of those. Reproducing the shells with placeholder numbers or
 * stock imagery would make this page a lie in exactly the shape the rest of this repository exists
 * to prevent, so those sections are absent rather than empty. Their typeface is not taken either —
 * the whole interface is monospace, and the owner asked for ours to be kept — and the caution in
 * the reference says why their 32px hero cannot simply be copied into a proportional face.
 *
 * Every claim below is one this repository has evidence for on 2026-09-20 — the consent gate, the
 * refusals, the visual critique, the plan limits — and the install line asks the shared constant
 * rather than asserting anything. The copy is unchanged by this pass. Structure and classes moved;
 * not one sentence did.
 */

const free = PLAN_LIMITS.free;

/* THE MODEL THE COMPOSER NAMES, ASKED RATHER THAN TYPED.
 * Reference claim 13: the most copyable object on the bar is a chip inside the composer that names
 * the model which will answer. Ours already has that object in the product — apps/web's composer
 * renders exactly this chip from PRODUCT_MODEL_INFO — so the landing reads the same constant
 * instead of repeating the word. A chip that names a model by hand is a chip that can disagree
 * with the picker it depicts. */
const composerModel = PRODUCT_MODEL_INFO[PRODUCT_MODELS[0]].name;

/* THE THREE SENTENCES THE HERO'S FIELD SHOWS.
 * They are examples of what a reader would TYPE, which is the only kind of content that can go in
 * that box without becoming a claim. Each one names only capabilities this repository has evidence
 * for on 2026-09-20 — instancing, scripts, checkpoints, the plugin's own write path — so a reader
 * who types the sentence back gets the thing the sentence describes. No counts, no timings, and no
 * "connected": those are assertions about state, and the picture of a product is read as its
 * behaviour.
 * THEY CYCLE, AND THE CYCLE IS THREE STACKED SPANS AND ONE @keyframes — NO SCRIPT HERE.
 * SHORT ON PURPOSE, AND THE LENGTHS ARE MEASURED. Each span is `white-space: nowrap`, because a
 * cross-fading line that rewraps halfway through the fade puts two different wraps on screen at
 * once. So every sentence must hold ONE line inside the width the field has at 375px. Rendered in
 * Chromium at 14/21 with this stack's own metrics, these three measure 166px, 208px and 199px.
 * The first draft of this list was sixty characters a line and was CLIPPED at 285px on every one —
 * the array had not been swapped when the comment above it already claimed it had, and the only
 * reason that is not live right now is that the number came off the rendered page rather than off
 * the source. Re-measure before editing a word of these. */
const composerExamples = [
  'Make a twelve-stage obby',
  'Add a shop that saves what I buy',
  'Build a lobby with a round timer',
];

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE SKY STRATA — COMPUTED HERE, ANIMATED IN THE SHEET, DRAWN OVER BY THE CANVAS.
 *
 * The owner's standing instruction is the most keyframes and the most running animations this page
 * has ever carried. The reference he named has ZERO of both, he was shown that measurement, and he
 * decided anyway — so this is not a contradiction to resolve, it is the brief. The way it stays
 * honest against the reference's own rule 17 ("buy the atmosphere with one piece of art direction,
 * not with a stack of layers") is that there is still ONE image here. It simply has a great many
 * moving parts inside it instead of being five mechanisms piled on each other.
 *
 * THAT ONE IMAGE IS A LANDSCAPE, AND THIS FILE DRAWS ONLY THE PART OF IT THE CANVAS DOES NOT.
 * <Horizon /> puts its horizon line at 0.74 of the viewport, paints the glow band on it, the
 * perspective grid below it and a sky of ninety drifting particles above it. So the division is:
 *
 *   the canvas  — the horizon, the floor, the glow, the particles
 *   this file   — the strata (long, near-flat haze bands in the sky) and the ridge on the horizon
 *
 * TWENTY EMBERS WERE WRITTEN HERE AND DELETED BEFORE THEY SHIPPED. They were small drifting dots
 * scattered above and below the fold — which is exactly the canvas's ninety sky particles, drawn a
 * second time in a second technology. Two mechanisms doing one job is the failure rule 17 names,
 * and adding them would have raised the element count while making the picture worse. The strata
 * stayed because the canvas draws no such thing; the embers went because it already does.
 *
 * DENSITY YIELDS TO THE COMPOSER, AND IT YIELDS IN THE DATA RATHER THAN IN A HOPE.
 * apps/web/tests/decoration-yields-to-the-composer.test.mjs records this the hard way: a mesh that
 * was densest directly behind the composer read, correctly, as broken. The hero's content column is
 * about 475px tall and centres in a 900px viewport, so it occupies roughly 24% to 76% of the field
 * and the composer itself sits at 51% to 65%. The strata are therefore NOT evenly spaced: their
 * position is y(t) = 0.38·t + 0.62·t^2.8 across the sky band, which packs them at the top of the
 * frame and opens them out as they descend, and their weight falls as (1 − t)^1.4 on top of that.
 *
 * Measured off the numbers this generator actually emits, as ink per 100px of band:
 *
 *   free sky, above the headline      3.07      adjacent gaps: 12px at the top of the frame
 *   the headline and its one line     0.57
 *   THE COMPOSER                      0.31      adjacent gaps: 66px at the bottom of the band
 *   the foot, down to the horizon     0.41
 *
 * The composer's band is the thinnest of the four and a tenth of the free sky's. That is a property
 * of the curve rather than of an eye, which is the only kind of yielding that survives an edit.
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
const STRATA_COUNT = 26;
const SKY_TOP = -150; // above the top of the frame: a stratum must enter from off-screen
const SKY_DEPTH = 805; // down to 655, which is just clear of the canvas's horizon at 0.74 × 900
const round = (n: number) => Math.round(n);
const strata = Array.from({ length: STRATA_COUNT }, (_, i) => {
  const t = i / (STRATA_COUNT - 1);
  const y = SKY_TOP + SKY_DEPTH * (0.38 * t + 0.62 * t ** 2.8);
  const weight = Number((0.16 + 0.84 * (1 - t) ** 1.4).toFixed(3));
  const amp = 30 + 90 * (1 - t); // the bow flattens as the band nears the horizon, as haze does
  const phase = ((i % 5) - 2) * 16;
  return {
    i,
    t: Number(t.toFixed(3)),
    weight,
    d: `M-140 ${round(y)} C 300 ${round(y - amp)}, 720 ${round(y + amp * 0.62 + phase)}, 1140 ${round(y - amp * 0.34)} S 1560 ${round(y + amp * 0.26 - phase)}, 1780 ${round(y - amp * 0.12)}`,
  };
});

const truths = [
  { label: 'Scope', title: 'One place, the one you opened', body: 'Apple works in the Studio place you pair it to, and nowhere else. It cannot reach your files or your other games.' },
  { label: 'Consent', title: 'Edits are off until you allow them', body: 'The plugin opens read-only. Turning writes on takes two deliberate clicks, and disconnecting turns them off again.' },
  { label: 'Refusals', title: 'It says no, by name', body: 'It will not run code it was sent, drive play mode, or insert an asset carrying a script. Each refusal comes back with its reason.' },
  /* "A RUN YOU DISLIKE IS A SINGLE CTRL+Z AWAY" WAS FALSE FOR EVERY RUN THAT WROTE MORE THAN ONCE.
     The title is true and stays: Commands.luau opens exactly one ChangeHistoryService recording per
     operation, and Bridge.luau walks a server batch one operation at a time. That is precisely why
     the old body could not be true — one waypoint per change means a five-step run is five presses,
     and a reader who pressed once got back the last write and kept the other four. The one-keystroke
     exit is the checkpoint, so that is what this now points at. */
  { label: 'Undo', title: 'Every change is one waypoint', body: 'Writes are wrapped in Studio’s own undo history — one step per change, so a five-step run is five presses of Ctrl+Z. To take a whole run back at once, restore the checkpoint Apple saved before it started.' },
];

// The activity figure is data, not a pasted blob: the refused line is the point of the whole
// figure and needs its own element to carry colour, and a string with markup spliced into it is
// how that ends up unparseable.
const activity = [
  //[[ `get_project_tree`, NOT `get_tree`. THIS LINE NAMED A TOOL THAT DOES NOT EXIST.
  //
  //   `get_tree` is the plugin's wire op — what apps/worker/src/tools.ts sends down the socket for
  //   the tool called `get_project_tree` (`studioOps: ['get_tree']`). No tool is registered under
  //   it, the workspace activity log cannot print it, and no user has ever seen it. The other six
  //   lines of this figure name real tools, which is precisely why it survived: five correct names
  //   and one internal protocol verb look identical to anyone not holding the registry open.
  //
  //   The column is unchanged. `get_tree` was padded to seventeen characters and
  //   `get_project_tree` is sixteen plus its single space, so the second column does not move.
  //
  //   tests/site-names-real-tools.test.mjs now compares every tool-shaped word on every built page
  //   against the names the worker advertises, so the next one is caught the day it is written.
  { text: 'get_project_tree game.Workspace           ok' },
  { text: 'create_instances 4 parts                  refused', refused: true },
  { text: '  writes require explicit edit consent', refused: true },
  { text: 'create_instances 4 parts                  ok' },
  { text: 'edit_script      ServerScriptService.Main ok' },
  { text: 'render_view      eye · 288×180            ok' },
  { text: 'inspect_visually score 6/10 · 3 defects   ok' },
];

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE FOUR CAPABILITIES, EACH WITH A SMALL WORKING THING RATHER THAN A PARAGRAPH.
 *
 * WHAT WAS HERE AND WHY IT WENT. Four `.card`s: a 16:9 tile holding one 40px line drawing at
 * --faint, then a title, then a muted sentence. Every claim on the page's most product-specific
 * section was a sentence asking to be believed, and the tile above it was an empty grey rectangle
 * with an icon in the middle of it. The owner's instruction is quoted in the ledger and is not
 * ambiguous: "Stop presenting major capabilities as plain text cards. Every important feature
 * should receive a small interactive experience of its own."
 *
 * SO EACH ONE IS NOW AN OBJECT YOU OPERATE, and `glyph` is gone because there is no glyph left.
 * The titles and the bodies below are UNCHANGED, to the character. Only what sits above them
 * moved. The copy on this page has been fought over and passed scripts/check-copy.mjs; a
 * restructure that also rewrites the sentences is two changes wearing one commit message.
 *
 * WHAT EACH STAGE IS ALLOWED TO CLAIM. This is the whole difficulty of the section and it is why
 * the heads exist. A widget on a marketing page is read as the product's behaviour — the status
 * dot that used to sit in the composer is the precedent, and it was removed for exactly that. So:
 *
 *   tree  — the ORDER OF OPERATIONS, driven by the reader. Reading a node prints the read; the
 *           write gate stays shut until three are read. That order is real: apps/worker walks the
 *           tree and takes a checkpoint before a write, and the gate here is that rule, not a
 *           connection to anybody's Studio.
 *   eye   — the SHAPE OF THE CRITIQUE. A score out of ten and named defects, which is what
 *           inspect_visually returns. The scene is drawn here and the three defects are written
 *           here; neither is a live render, and the head says the stage is a sample.
 *   code  — the SHAPE OF THE OUTPUT AND OF THE REFUSAL. Two asks produce Luau, the third produces
 *           the refusal by name. The refusal is the point of the stage and is the one thing on it
 *           that is quoted from the plugin rather than composed for the page.
 *   cube  — A MESH YOU CAN TURN. It is authored here. It does not claim to have been generated,
 *           and the head calls it a sample so that nothing else has to.
 *
 * `demo` is the hook the script in this file switches on. `stage` is the label above each one.
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
const capabilities = [
  { title: 'Reads before it writes', body: 'It walks your instance tree and scripts first, then proposes the change — and takes a checkpoint before touching anything.', demo: 'tree', stage: 'Try it · read order' },
  { title: 'Checks its own work', body: 'It renders the scene from inside Studio and scores the picture against a visual gate, with named defects rather than a verdict.', demo: 'eye', stage: 'Sample critique' },
  { title: 'Builds in Luau, not pseudocode', body: 'Scripts land in your place through Studio’s own editor APIs, and it refuses to write code that loads assets or calls the network.', demo: 'code', stage: 'Try it · ask for three things' },
  { title: 'Generates real geometry', body: 'Text-to-3D runs through Roblox’s own generation service, and a descendant scan refuses anything that arrives carrying code.', demo: 'cube', stage: 'Sample mesh · drag to turn' },
];

/* THE INSTANCE TREE THE FIRST STAGE READS.
 * Four services and the children that make the read meaningful, in the order Studio's own Explorer
 * shows them. `kids` is what the read reports back, so the log line has a fact in it rather than a
 * tick. Nothing here is anybody's place: it is the empty-baseplate shape every new Studio file
 * opens with, which is the one tree a reader of this page can check against their own screen. */
const readTree = [
  { path: 'Workspace', kids: 2, depth: 0 },
  { path: 'Baseplate', kids: 0, depth: 1 },
  { path: 'SpawnLocation', kids: 1, depth: 1 },
  { path: 'ServerScriptService', kids: 1, depth: 0 },
  { path: 'Main', kids: 0, depth: 1 },
  { path: 'ReplicatedStorage', kids: 0, depth: 0 },
];

/* THE THREE DEFECTS THE SECOND STAGE NAMES.
 * `at` is the marker's position on the 220x124 render as a percentage, so the marker and the list
 * item cannot drift apart: one array drives both. The wording is the visual gate's own register —
 * a defect names a thing that is wrong, not a grade. */
const defects = [
  { id: 'd1', at: [22, 68], text: 'Floor is one flat slab — no material break' },
  { id: 'd2', at: [58, 34], text: 'Two towers share an edge and read as one' },
  { id: 'd3', at: [82, 72], text: 'Nothing marks where a player spawns' },
];

/* THE THREE ASKS AND WHAT COMES BACK.
 * The first two are Luau. The third is the refusal, and its wording is the plugin's: writes that
 * fetch across the network are refused by name and with a reason. `refused` is what colours it. */
const asks = [
  {
    label: 'A leaderstat',
    lines: [
      'local Players = game:GetService("Players")',
      '',
      'Players.PlayerAdded:Connect(function(player)',
      '  local stats = Instance.new("Folder")',
      '  stats.Name = "leaderstats"',
      '  local coins = Instance.new("IntValue")',
      '  coins.Name = "Coins"',
      '  coins.Parent = stats',
      '  stats.Parent = player',
      'end)',
    ],
  },
  {
    label: 'Save what a player buys',
    lines: [
      'local DataStoreService = game:GetService("DataStoreService")',
      'local store = DataStoreService:GetDataStore("Purchases")',
      '',
      'local function save(userId, owned)',
      '  local ok, err = pcall(function()',
      '    store:SetAsync("u_" .. userId, owned)',
      '  end)',
      '  if not ok then warn("save failed: " .. tostring(err)) end',
      'end',
    ],
  },
  {
    label: 'Fetch a script from a URL',
    refused: true,
    lines: [
      'refused  edit_script',
      '',
      '  A script that calls out to the network is not',
      '  written. The reason is given rather than the',
      '  request being dropped: code fetched at runtime',
      '  cannot be reviewed before it runs in your place.',
      '',
      '  Nothing was written.',
    ],
  },
];
---

<Landing
  title="Apple — build Roblox games inside Studio"
  description="Describe what you want, and Apple builds it in the Roblox Studio place you already have open. It reads before it writes, checks its own work, and edits nothing until you allow it."
>
  <a href="#main" class="skip-link">Skip to content</a>

  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="shell">
      <a href="/" class="wordmark" aria-label="Apple — home"><AppleMark size={22} /><span>Apple</span></a>
      <nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Primary">
        <a href="/docs">Docs</a>
        <a href="/pricing">Pricing</a>
        <a href="/changelog">Changelog</a>
        <a href="/status">Status</a>
      </nav>
      {/* THE ONE CONTROL THIS PAGE WAS MISSING. Every other route carries a theme toggle in Nav.astro;
          the landing has its own header and had none, so a reader who chose light elsewhere on the
          site could not see or change that choice on the front page. It lands on one of the four
          control heights the reference quantises to (claim 10), like everything beside it. */}
      <div class="header-actions">
      {/* SIGN IN WAS NOWHERE ON THIS PAGE, at any width. "Open Apple" does land on the sign-in
          screen, but nothing said so — a returning customer had to guess. Every other page on
          this site carries a Sign in; the front door did not.
          IT SAT IN THE DESTINATIONS ROW UNTIL IT WAS MEASURED ON A PHONE, and there it was not
          merely cramped, it was unreachable: at 375px `nav.site-nav` ran scrollWidth 395 against
          clientWidth 343, and this link's own box was x 340->411 — 36px past the right edge, with
          `scrollbar-width: none` above removing the only hint that the row scrolls at all. The
          block at the 640px breakpoint predicted "310px ... fits with room to spare"; the
          measurement says 395, so that arithmetic was wrong rather than outgrown.
          It moves here because that is what it IS. Docs, Pricing, Changelog and Status are
          destinations; signing in is an account action, and it belongs beside the other one
          rather than at the end of a list of pages. Removing it returns 75px (71px box + the 4px
          gap) to a row that overflowed by 52, which is what makes the four that remain fit. */}
      <a class="header-signin" href="/app/login">Sign in</a>
      <button class="pill pill-quiet pill-icon" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="true" aria-label="Switch to light theme">
        <svg class="icon-sun" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" aria-hidden="true">
          <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="4.2" fill="currentColor"></circle>
          <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round">
            <path d="M12 2.6v2.2M12 19.2v2.2M2.6 12h2.2M19.2 12h2.2M5.3 5.3l1.6 1.6M17.1 17.1l1.6 1.6M18.7 5.3l-1.6 1.6M6.9 17.1l-1.6 1.6"></path>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <svg class="icon-moon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M20 14.4A8.4 8.4 0 0 1 9.6 4a8.4 8.4 0 1 0 10.4 10.4Z" fill="currentColor"></path>
        </svg>
      </button>
      <a class="pill" href="/app">Open Apple</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main id="main">
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      {/* THE ATMOSPHERE IS ONE CONTAINER AND IT HOLDS EVERYTHING THAT MOVES ON ITS OWN.
          Reference claim 3: the bar's entire atmosphere is a single fixed layer at z-index -1,
          100lvw by 100lvh, with all content above it. Ours is authored here because this is where
          its geometry is computed, and landing.css is what makes it fixed — a fixed child of the
          hero covers the viewport for the whole page, which is the shape the claim describes.
          It is `aria-hidden` because it is decoration: nothing in it is content, and a screen
          reader that announced twenty-six streamlines would be reading scenery aloud. */}
      <div class="atmosphere" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="light-column"></div>
        {/* <Horizon /> replaces the flow field that used to sit here. The canvas is ADDITIVE and
            everything under it is a finished composition without it — the light column and the
            line art below are what a visitor sees if the script is blocked, throws, or never
            reaches its element, which is not hypothetical: exactly that shipped to production on
            this page and every reading of the served HTML said the effect was there. */}
        <Horizon />
        <svg class="sky" viewBox="0 0 1600 900" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice">
          {/* Each band carries `--i` (its ordinal) and `--t` (how far down the sky it sits), so the
              sheet can phase ONE keyframe across all twenty-six with calc() rather than authoring
              twenty-six rules. The `opacity` attribute is the computed weight and is a presentation
              attribute on purpose: it is the lowest-priority way to say it, so any rule the sheet
              writes for `.stratum` wins over it, and the field is still correctly graded on the day
              before that rule exists. */}
          <g class="strata">
            {strata.map((band) => (
              <path class="stratum" style={`--i:${band.i};--t:${band.t}`} opacity={band.weight} d={band.d} />
            ))}
          </g>
          {/* THE RIDGE, ON THE HORIZON AND NEVER BELOW IT. Every vertex here is between y=578 and
              y=666, and 666 is 0.74 × 900 — the exact line the canvas draws its glow band on. A
              ridge whose feet fell below that would be a mountain standing on a perspective floor,
              which is two incompatible readings of the same ground plane. `.ridge` is the hook
              landing.css already animates by name; renaming it would leave a live rule pointing at
              nothing, which is how a stylesheet fills up with rules nobody can delete. */}
          <path class="ridge" style="--i:0;--t:0.94" d="M-40 666 L180 622 L330 654 L520 616 L700 660 L900 620 L1120 656 L1340 612 L1560 658 L1840 630" />
          <path class="ridge" style="--i:1;--t:0.97" d="M-40 664 L240 592 L430 648 L640 578 L860 646 L1090 590 L1330 650 L1600 584 L1840 640" />
        </svg>
      </div>

      <div class="shell hero-inner">
        {/* THE EYEBROW. Claim 11: labels on the bar are uppercase with positive tracking against a
            body that is neither, and that is how a page gets a third level of hierarchy without a
            second typeface — which matters more for us than for them, because we are keeping a
            proportional face where they have a monospace one. `.micro` is already that object. */}
        <p class="micro hero-eyebrow">Inside Roblox Studio</p>
        <h1 id="hero-title" class="display">Build it in the place you already have open.</h1>
        <p class="lead">Describe the game you want. Apple builds it inside your own Roblox Studio place, names every step while it happens, and changes nothing until you say so.</p>

        {/* THE COMPOSER AND THE ROW UNDER IT ARE ONE BLOCK, and the composer is the focal object of
            the hero rather than a screenshot of one. Splitting the group into siblings with their
            own margins is how the gaps between the three drifted apart the first time. */}
        <div class="composer-group">
          {/*[[ THE COMPOSER IS THE PRODUCT'S OWN CONTROL NOW, NOT A PICTURE OF IT.
            *
            *   It carried `aria-hidden="true"` and held three <span>s. The largest object above
            *   the fold on the front page was a photograph of a text field: a keyboard user could
            *   not reach it, a screen reader was told nothing was there, and a reader who clicked
            *   into it to type their idea — which is the single most likely thing anyone does with
            *   a box that looks like that — got nothing at all.
            *
            *   It is a real <form> with a real <textarea> and a real submit, and the submit goes
            *   where the page's own primary action already goes: /app/signup. What was typed
            *   travels with it as `?start=`, so the reader's sentence is not thrown away between
            *   the front door and the first screen inside.
            *
            *   NOTHING IN THE APP READS `start` YET, and that is written here rather than left to
            *   be discovered, because this repository has exactly that scar: `data-cursor` sat on
            *   three elements for weeks with no renderer, and every reader of those files
            *   reasonably concluded a cursor system existed. docs/backlog/HANDOFF-HERO-START.md
            *   names the hop and who has to build it. The page claims nothing about a prefill; it
            *   says "Create a free account", which is what happens.
            *
            *   THE THREE EXAMPLES SURVIVE, as the empty state. They are the same three sentences
            *   on the same one keyframe with no script; they are now a ghost behind a real field
            *   and CSS hides them the moment it is focused or holds a character. Deleting them
            *   would have removed the only moving thing in the foreground of this page. ]]*/}
          <form class="composer" action="/app/signup" method="get">
            {/* All three sentences are in the markup, stacked, and the stylesheet fades between
                them. With motion turned off the cycle does not stop on whichever one happened to
                be painted — a cancelled animation returns to the declared value, so the stylesheet
                declares the first line visible and that is the still state. Every line is a
                finished sentence either way; none of them is a fragment. */}
            <div class="composer-field">
              <label class="visually-hidden" for="hero-start">What do you want built in your Roblox Studio place?</label>
              {/* `placeholder=" "` is a single space and is deliberate: it makes `:placeholder-shown`
                  true while the field is empty, which is what lets the ghost below be hidden by CSS
                  alone with no script. A visible placeholder would be a second empty-state message
                  competing with the three sentences. */}
              <textarea
                id="hero-start"
                name="start"
                class="composer-input"
                rows="1"
                maxlength="280"
                placeholder=" "
                autocomplete="off"
                spellcheck="false"
              ></textarea>
              {/* The ghost. aria-hidden because the field above it is the real object and a screen
                  reader reading three cycling example sentences into a text field is noise. */}
              <span class="composer-typed" aria-hidden="true">
                {composerExamples.map((example, i) => (
                  <span class="composer-line" style={`--turn:${i}`}>{example}</span>
                ))}
              </span>
            </div>
            <div class="composer-bar">
              {/* THE MODEL CHIP, WHICH IS THE ONE OBJECT THE REFERENCE SAYS TO COPY OUTRIGHT.
                  Claim 13: a 32px, 6px-radius chip filled at 12% of the accent, bordered at 38% of
                  the lighter tint and glowing at 14% — sitting in the composer beside the
                  attachment control, naming the model that will answer. Ours names it from the
                  shared constant, so it is the same word the product's own picker shows.

                  THERE IS NO STATUS DOT, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. What used to sit here was a green
                  dot and the word "connected", on a page selling a product whose plugin Roblox has
                  not approved for distribution — the largest, most tappable-looking thing above the
                  fold on a phone told a reader the product was attached to their Studio before they
                  had one, and the sub-line two blocks down said the opposite. A reader believes the
                  picture. The accent is spent on the model's NAME, which is a fact about the
                  product, and on nothing that could be read as a state. */}
              <div class="composer-chips">
                {/* THE ATTACHMENT CONTROL COMES FIRST AND THE MODEL CHIP IS LAST, AND THAT ORDER IS
                    LOAD-BEARING. landing.css spends the whole accent family on this row through
